*kkiyama117-nvim-lsp.txt*

Author: kkiyama117
License: NYSL

==============================================================================
About

LSP setup of this vimrc: kakehashi is the SOLE Neovim LSP client; every
other language server (denols, emmylua_ls, gopls, pyright, rust_analyzer,
tombi, vtsls) runs as a bridged CHILD process of kakehashi.

The layout follows atusy's dotfiles (atusy/kakehashi.nvim +
atusy/kakehashi-lspconfig + after/lsp/kakehashi.lua) and is described in
detail below.

Contents: >
	OVERVIEW
	FILES
	CLIENT CONFIG
	SERVER CONFIG
	BRIDGED SERVERS
	DPP FILETYPE
	HIGHLIGHTING HANDOFF
	DIAGNOSTICS
	MAINTENANCE
<

==============================================================================
OVERVIEW                                    *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-overview*

- kakehashi (https://github.com/atusy/kakehashi) is a Rust LSP server that
  speaks LSP to nvim and forwards to downstream language servers, bridging
  them into a single client.
- The nvim-side plugin is upstream atusy/kakehashi.nvim (installed by dpp,
  see |kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-files|).
- kakehashi-lspconfig (atusy/kakehashi-lspconfig) is NOT a Neovim plugin:
  it ships `lsp/*.toml` fragments (converted from nvim-lspconfig) that
  describe each downstream server for kakehashi.
- LSP server binaries are installed by mise (~/.config/mise/config.toml),
  NOT mason.nvim.
- There is no inherit_nvim_lsp_config and no didChangeConfiguration: the
  downstream servers are configured BEFORE kakehashi spawns, via
  `--config-file` layers (see |kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-server-config|).

==============================================================================
FILES                                           *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-files*

after/lsp/kakehashi.lua
	Neovim client config. Loaded lazily by nvim when
	`vim.lsp.config['kakehashi']` is first accessed (nvim resolves
	`lsp/*.lua` from the runtimepath; `after/` is searched last and wins
	over nvim-lspconfig's builtin lsp/kakehashi.lua). Builds the
	--config-file stack and returns the `vim.lsp.Config` table.

lua/hooks/kakehashi.nvim.dpp
	dpp hooks_file (|kkiyama117-nvim|). Only calls
	`vim.lsp.enable('kakehashi')` — enable eagerly resolves the config,
	which triggers the after/lsp load, so it must NOT be called inside
	after/lsp/kakehashi.lua (that would recurse).

lua/vimrc/kakehashi_config.lua
	vimrc-owned data: `bridged_servers`, per-server `filetypes()`, and
	`languages()` (the init_options.languages bridge table).

kakehashi/kakehashi.toml
	Server-side config (enables + per-server settings). Symlinked from
	the kakehashi server's standard user location
	~/.config/kakehashi/kakehashi.toml, so shell CLIs such as
	`kakehashi format` / `kakehashi diagnose` use the same config.
	See |kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-server-config|.

deps/nvim/lsp.toml
	dpp plugin entries: nvim-lspconfig (with its own hooks_file),
	kakehashi-lspconfig, and kakehashi.nvim (lazy on FileType;
	`depends = ["kakehashi-lspconfig"]` guarantees the fragments are on
	rtp when the client config resolves; `external_commands =
	["kakehashi"]`).

lua/hooks/nvim-lspconfig.dpp
	Per-server `vim.lsp.config` definitions (denols, emmylua_ls, vtsls,
	`*` capabilities, diagnostics/format config). Never enabled directly;
	their settings are mirrored into kakehashi/kakehashi.toml.

Generated at runtime under $NVIM_CACHE_HOME/kakehashi/ >
	lsp.toml	concatenated fragments (see below)
	library.toml	emmylua workspace.library for the running nvim
<

==============================================================================
CLIENT CONFIG                                *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-client*

after/lsp/kakehashi.lua returns a `vim.lsp.Config` with:

cmd
	kakehashi plus the --config-file stack
	(|kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-server-config|).

filetypes
	`config.filetypes()` from lua/kakehashi/config.lua: markdown family
	(markdown, markdown_inline, quarto, rmd) plus each bridged server's
	filetypes (incl. `dpp` via emmylua_ls).

init_options.searchPaths
	- ~/.local/share/kakehashi (server data dir)
	- $NVIM_CONFIG_HOME/kakehashi (repo-owned parser + queries)
	- the kakehashi.nvim plugin rtp entry (bundled queries)

init_options.languages
	`config.languages()`: host-bridge (`bridge._self`) for go,
	javascript, lua, python, rust, toml, typescript; markdown/quarto/rmd
	aggregation priorities; pull-only diagnostics sealing
	(|kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-diagnostics|); the `dpp` route
	(|kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-dpp-filetype|).

on_init
	Sets `semanticTokensProvider.range = false` to prefer full/delta
	token requests.

on_attach
	Highlighting handoff with the dpp exception
	(|kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-handoff|).

==============================================================================
SERVER CONFIG                                *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-server-config*

kakehashi is spawned with three --config-file layers, merged in order
(later overrides earlier; per-server settings/initializationOptions are
deep-merged — see src/config/merge.rs of the kakehashi server):

1. lsp.toml (generated) >
	kakehashi-lspconfig fragments, filtered to `bridged_servers`.
	Each fragment's `cmd` first binary is resolved via
	`vim.fn.exepath()` so mise-installed binaries are found even when
	nvim was launched without mise on PATH.
<
2. kakehashi.toml (repo) >
	Fragments ship with `enabled = false`; this file sets
	`enabled = true` for exactly the bridged servers, and carries the
	per-server settings (denols lint/root markers, emmylua
	semanticTokens/runtime/diagnostics, ...).
<
3. library.toml (generated) >
	Dynamic emmylua `workspace.library` (vim.env.VIMRUNTIME + nvim
	runtime lua dirs). Kept a FLAT array of paths — an invalid
	EmmyrcWorkspacePathItem makes emmylua fall back to defaults
	(re-introducing `undefined global variable: vim`).
<

==============================================================================
BRIDGED SERVERS                            *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-servers*

Server          Filetypes                              Notes >
	denols          javascript, javascriptreact,          markers deno.json,
	                typescript, typescriptreact           deno.jsonc, deps.ts
	emmylua_ls      lua, dpp                              LuaJIT, globals
	                                                      vim; workspace.library
	                                                      generated at runtime
	gopls           go, gomod, gowork, gotmpl
	pyright         python
	rust_analyzer   rust
	tombi           toml
	vtsls           javascript, javascriptreact,
	                typescript, typescriptreact
<

Keep `bridged_servers` in lua/kakehashi/config.lua in sync with the
`enabled` entries in kakehashi/kakehashi.toml and the mise installs in
~/.config/mise/config.toml.

==============================================================================
DPP FILETYPE                                *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-dpp-filetype*

`*.dpp` files (dpp.vim hooks files) are handled specially:

- kakehashi attaches via `filetypes()` (dpp is listed under
  emmylua_ls's filetypes).
- The custom tree-sitter parser at
  $NVIM_CONFIG_HOME/kakehashi/parser/dpp.so parses hook blocks; the
  queries (kakehashi/queries/dpp/injections.scm) inject each block's
  content as lua, and the injected virtual documents are bridged to
  emmylua_ls — the only path that reaches emmylua (it keys documents by
  URI extension).
- Tree-sitter keeps highlighting: semantic tokens are disabled for dpp
  buffers ONLY, in on_attach. after/ftplugin/dpp.lua starts tree-sitter
  (lua parser + luadoc injections) and sets up folding.
- Rationale: kakehashi's tokens for dpp are only host markers; injected
  lua tokens depend on emmylua's slow analysis, so the token-based
  takeover would leave the buffer uncolored.

==============================================================================
HIGHLIGHTING HANDOFF                        *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-handoff*

For every non-dpp buffer, kakehashi owns highlighting once semantic
tokens arrive: on the first LspTokenUpdate, syntax is turned OFF and
tree-sitter is stopped for that buffer. >

	on_attach = function(client, bufnr)
	  if vim.bo[bufnr].filetype == 'dpp' then
	    vim.lsp.semantic_tokens.enable(false, { bufnr = bufnr,
	      client_id = client.id })
	    return
	  end
	  vim.api.nvim_create_autocmd('LspTokenUpdate', {
	    buffer = bufnr,
	    once = true,
	    callback = function()
	      vim.opt_local.syntax = 'OFF'
	      vim.treesitter.stop(bufnr)
	    end,
	  })
	end
<

==============================================================================
DIAGNOSTICS                                *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-diagnostics*

- Diagnostics are pull-only: `textDocument/publishDiagnostics` is sealed
  (priorities = {}) in `languages._.layers.aggregation`, so nvim gets a
  single set via `textDocument/diagnostic` (kakehashi folds the push
  servers' cached diagnostics into pull answers).
- Because the downstream servers are configured at spawn
  (--config-file), there is no didChangeConfiguration race — the
  re-pull workaround of the old fork (polling effectiveConfiguration)
  is unnecessary.

==============================================================================
MAINTENANCE                                  *kkiyama117-nvim-lsp-maintenance*

Adding a server: >
	1. Install the binary via mise.
	2. Add the name to `bridged_servers` in
	   lua/kakehashi/config.lua (and `server_filetypes`).
	3. Add `[languageServers.<name>] enabled = true` (and settings) to
	   kakehashi/kakehashi.toml.
	4. A fragment `lsp/<name>.toml` must exist in kakehashi-lspconfig;
	   if not, write the server table yourself into
	   kakehashi/kakehashi.toml (cmd, languages, workspaceMarkers).
<

Updating plugins: >
	:DppUpdate
<

Notes:

- Editing any file under $NVIM_CONFIG_HOME (lua/toml/dpp) triggers dpp's
  BufWritePost watcher: state rebuild (and install for toml changes).
- On a fresh clone, recreate the symlink:
  ~/.config/kakehashi/kakehashi.toml -> kakehashi/kakehashi.toml
  (the client warns when it is missing).

